{"name":"Philodendron","hex":"#116356","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"philodendron","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several climbing plants, of the genus Philodendron, native to America and the West Indies that are often grown as house plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/philodendron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Philodendron","blocks":[{"text":"Philodendron is a large genus of flowering plants in the family Araceae. As of September 2025, the Plants of the World Online accepted 625 species; other sources accept different numbers. Regardless of number of species, the genus is the second-largest member of the family Araceae, after genus Anthurium. Taxonomically, the genus Philodendron is still poorly known, with many undescribed species. Many are grown as ornamental and indoor plants. The name derives from the Greek words philo- 'love, affection' and dendron 'tree'. The generic name, Philodendron, is often used as the English name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Philodendron_giganteum_-_Laeken_Royal_Greenhouses_-_Royal_Castle_of_Laeken_-_Brussels%2C_Belgium_-_DSC07236.jpg/330px-Philodendron_giganteum_-_Laeken_Royal_Greenhouses_-_Royal_Castle_of_Laeken_-_Brussels%2C_Belgium_-_DSC07236.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philodendron","wikidata":"Q131880"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/PhilodendronMaximum.jpg/500px-PhilodendronMaximum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/PhilodendronMaximum.jpg/1280px-PhilodendronMaximum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/PhilodendronMaximum.jpg/500px-PhilodendronMaximum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=